To set up a system that automatically populates a Google Sheet with event information from your Google Calendar, you can use Google Apps Script to create a custom script that syncs the two. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you set this up:

1. Create a new Google Sheet where you'd like to store the event information.
2. Click on "Extensions" in the menu bar, then click on "Apps Script."
3. In the Google Apps Script editor, replace any existing code with the following code:
```javascript
function syncCalendarToSheet() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet().getActiveSheet();
  var calendar = CalendarApp.getDefaultCalendar();
  var events = calendar.getEvents(new Date(), new Date(new Date().getTime() + 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000));

  // Clear existing content and add headers
  sheet.clearContents();
  sheet.appendRow(["Event Title", "Start Time", "End Time", "Description", "Location"]);

  events.forEach(function(event) {
    var title = event.getTitle();
    var startTime = event.getStartTime();
    var endTime = event.getEndTime();
    var description = event.getDescription();
    var location = event.getLocation();
    sheet.appendRow([title, startTime, endTime, description, location]);
  });
}
```
This script will sync events from your default Google Calendar for the next 30 days into the Google Sheet. You can customize the date range by modifying the `new Date()` and `new Date(new Date().getTime() + 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000)` parts of the code.

1. Save the script by clicking the floppy disk icon or pressing `CTRL+S` (or `CMD+S` on macOS).
2. To run the script manually, click on the "Select function" dropdown in the toolbar, choose `syncCalendarToSheet`, and then click the triangular "Run" button. This will run the script and populate your Google Sheet with event information from your calendar.
3. To automate the script, go to "Triggers" in the left sidebar (it looks like a clock), and then click the blue "+ Add Trigger" button in the bottom right corner.
4. Configure the trigger settings as follows:
* Choose which function to run: `syncCalendarToSheet`
* Choose which deployment should run: `Head`
* Event source: `Time-driven`
* Type of time-based trigger: Choose your preferred frequency (e.g., "Hour timer" to run the script every hour, "Day timer" to run it daily, etc.)
* Time of day or interval: Choose your preferred time or interval
1. Click "Save" to save the trigger. The script will now run automatically at the specified interval.

Remember that you can customize the script to store event information in specific columns or format the data as needed.

